


In the back seat, Jonah leaned his head against the window. For the first ten minutes he said nothing. Then he asked, “Who has the cards?”




“I do.”




“The handheld too?”




Maren held out her hand.




He looked at it.




“Our agreement,” she said. “No release, upload, copy, or edit before joint review. That includes tonight.”




“I wasn’t going to upload anything.”




“Then give it to me.”




He took the recorder from his pocket and placed it in her palm.




Maren removed the memory card, put it in a labeled sleeve, and returned the recorder.




Jonah watched the sleeve disappear into the case. “And the footage from my house?”




“Still separate. Still private.”




He turned back to the window.




Lena drove.




By the time they reached Jonah’s townhouse, the bleeding had not come through the second bandage. Maren made him drink water, leave the equipment in the car, and text his sister that he was home. He complied with each instruction. At the door he stopped with one hand on the frame.




“Don’t decide what happened before I see the files,” he said.




“I won’t.”




“Don’t decide it didn’t happen either.”




Maren looked at the bandage around his right hand. “Sleep.”




He went inside.




At eight twelve the next morning, Ruth placed Jonah’s phone on the preserve-office counter inside a clear zipper bag.




Mud streaked one corner of the case. The screen was black. A small leaf fragment clung beneath the camera bump.




“I found it where you said,” Ruth told Maren. “On a rectangular stone above marker four. I approached from the staff route, not from your track. I photographed it before I touched anything.”




Maren stood on the visitor side of the counter. Jonah had arrived five minutes earlier wearing a clean bandage, the same jeans and boots, and a clean shirt. He looked past the phone toward Ruth.




“What is the stone?” he asked.




“I don’t know.”




“It’s not on your map.”




“The public map is not an inventory of every foundation remnant, boundary feature, old work surface, quarry fragment, or piece of masonry in the preserve.” Ruth set her own phone beside the bag and opened a photograph. “There’s no inscription I could see. No marked plot. No sign. I’m sending the location to our resource staff because you were off trail near a protected zone, not because I think you found a grave.”




Jonah looked at the photograph. “It’s shaped like one.”




“Lots of things are rectangular.”




Maren kept her attention on the sealed bag.




“Did you hear anything when you went up?” Jonah asked.




Ruth met his eyes. “A woodpecker, traffic, and something moving in brush that I never saw. I was there in daylight.”




Jonah looked at Maren, then back at the photograph.




Maren asked, “Did the phone move before you picked it up?”




“Not while I was there. It was faceup, close to the middle. I took three photos, then bagged it. I did not unlock it.”




“Thank you.”




Ruth slid a short written note across the counter with the recovery time, location description, and her initials. Maren photographed the note and the sealed bag before Jonah opened it.




The phone would not turn on until they connected it to a charger in the editing room.




They drove there separately.




At nine sixteen, the phone showed three percent battery and a saved voice memo dated the previous night.




Jonah sat beside Maren at the workstation. The raw cards remained in their sleeves on the desk. She had copied nothing. The phone stayed connected by cable so it would not die during transfer.




“Don’t play it yet,” she said.




“I just want to see if it saved.”




“It saved.”




The voice-memo list showed one file beginning shortly before midnight.




Maren opened the file information rather than the waveform.




Duration: 24:18.




Jonah leaned closer. “That’s not right.”




“How long did you think you recorded?”




“Five minutes. Maybe six before you got there. Then however long we were at the stone.”




Maren checked her camera log. Her last recorded clip at the stone ended before they started downhill. The camera time and phone time were not exact enough to align by the displayed clocks, but they could still compare elapsed time without matching the clocks exactly.




She opened the voice memo and moved the playhead only far enough to find their departure. Jonah’s questions came through first. Her own voice followed, close to the phone. Then handling noise, their argument about leaving, branches, footsteps receding downslope.




She stopped playback when their voices had become too faint to distinguish words.




The file still had more than eleven minutes remaining.




Jonah stared at the untouched length of waveform to the right of the playhead.




Neither of them reached for the space bar.




Scene 9 — The Cut We Keep




At nine twenty-three, Maren pressed the space bar.




The phone memo resumed with the last trace of their retreat. Jonah’s voice had already lost its words. A branch snapped somewhere below the microphone, followed by several smaller contacts through leaves. Their footfalls continued downhill for another few seconds, then faded beneath the insect noise.




Maren stopped playback.




Jonah sat beside her with both forearms on the workstation. The fresh bandage on his right hand was clean. The phone remained connected to the charger between them, its screen awake but dimmed. Behind it, five memory-card sleeves stood in a row where Maren had placed them according to the equipment log.




“I thought we were listening to the whole thing,” he said.




“We are. After I make a copy.”




“You said no copies before joint review.”




“This is the joint review.”




She showed him the empty project folder on the external drive, then created a subfolder marked RAW — RESTRICTED. She copied the voice memo without renaming it and wrote the original filename, duration, phone battery state, Ruth’s recovery time, and the transfer time into the custody log. Jonah watched each entry.




When the copy finished, Maren disconnected the phone from the computer but left it charging from the wall. She opened the duplicated file in the editor and turned off automatic transcription.




Jonah noticed. “Why?”




“Because I don’t want the software putting words in our heads before we listen.”




He leaned back.




Maren returned to the beginning.




They listened once without stopping.




For the first several minutes, the recording captured Jonah almost continuously. His breathing, his boots through leaves, the scrape of his radio against clothing. He spoke to the dark while he moved off the white route. Some phrases were clear. Others broke when the phone shifted in his hand. He described hearing movement ahead, then behind him. He said he could see a shape through the trees, although nothing in the audio could establish what he was looking at. When he set the phone on the stone, the microphone struck the surface with one hard, flat contact.




Maren glanced at the radio log beside the keyboard.




Jonah heard the same thing. “That could be the hit on the radio.”




“It could.”




The timing was close enough to make the phone placement a viable source, but the devices did not share a stable clock offset. The entry slate from the shelter gave Maren a starting comparison. Later files showed the camera and handheld recorder drifting by different amounts. She could place events in order. She could not align every impact to the same second across all devices.
